---
import rawData from '../../data/deployment-topics.json';
import TopicLayout from '../../components/TopicLayout.astro';
import ScoreBar from '../../components/ScoreBar.astro';
import { type DeploymentData, type DeploymentRow, intervalPct, modelLabel, pct, pp, intervalPp } from '../../data/topics';

const data = rawData as unknown as DeploymentData;
const rows = data.rows.filter(row => row.track === 'wearable-sensor-transfer');
const contrasts = data.paired_contrasts.filter(row => row.track === 'wearable-sensor-transfer');
const citation = data.dataset_citations.find(item => item.id === 'zhu2021-wearable102-author-snapshot-20260920');
const find = (model: string, source: string | null, target: string) => rows.find(row => row.model === model && row.source_sensor === source && row.target_sensor === target);
const analyticModels = ['same-frequency-power', 'cca', 'fbcca'];
const trainedModels = ['spectral-ridge-gain-invariant', 'eegnet'];
const renderMetric = (row?: DeploymentRow) => row ? `${pct(row.value)} · 95% interval ${intervalPct(row.confidence_interval_95)}` : '—';
const wetDrop = contrasts.find(row => row.model === 'eegnet' && row.comparison.startsWith('wet-source'))!;
const dryGain = contrasts.find(row => row.model === 'eegnet' && row.comparison.startsWith('dry-source'))!;
---
<TopicLayout
  title="Dry vs. wet electrodes"
  description="Native eight-channel dry and wet SSVEP recordings compared across analytic references and participant-disjoint source-trained models."
  path="/topics/dry-vs-wet/"
  eyebrow="Sensor transfer · 102 participants"
  dek="Wet and dry recordings are not interchangeable inputs. The clearest mismatch appears when one EEGNet is trained on wet recordings and evaluated on dry recordings from held-out people—but sensor type is entangled with session order and time on task."
  activeSlug="dry-vs-wet"
>
  <section class="topic-section" aria-labelledby="transfer-finding">
    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-number"><strong>{pp(wetDrop.difference, 1)}</strong><span>Wet-trained EEGNet: dry minus wet balanced accuracy</span></div>
      <div class="finding-copy"><h3 id="transfer-finding">The largest observed cross-sensor loss</h3><p>Across the same 102 people, the paired participant bootstrap interval is {intervalPp(wetDrop.paired_participant_bootstrap_95, 1)}. This is a descriptive transfer result from one training seed, not a pure causal estimate of electrode hardware.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="topic-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Source-trained models</p><h2>Train on one sensor, test on both</h2></div><p>Six folds hold out 17 people at a time. Both sensor recordings from each held-out person stay out of source training. Every cell is participant-mean balanced accuracy on 12-target, two-second SSVEP.</p></div>
    <div class="topic-table scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Source-trained wet and dry sensor transfer results, scrolls horizontally">
      <table>
        <caption>Native 8-channel headband · 102 participants · 12,240 trials per target sensor. Parentheses give participant bootstrap 95% intervals.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Model and training source</th><th scope="col">Test on wet</th><th scope="col">Test on dry</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{trainedModels.flatMap(model => ['wet','dry'].map(source => {
          const wet = find(model, source, 'wet');
          const dry = find(model, source, 'dry');
          return <tr><th scope="row">{modelLabel(model)}<small>trained on {source} · participant-disjoint</small></th><td><span class="metric">{wet ? pct(wet.value) : '—'}</span><span class="interval">{wet ? intervalPct(wet.confidence_interval_95) : '—'}</span></td><td><span class="metric">{dry ? pct(dry.value) : '—'}</span><span class="interval">{dry ? intervalPct(dry.confidence_interval_95) : '—'}</span></td></tr>;
        }))}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="evidence-grid">
      <article class="evidence-card"><p class="eyebrow">Wet-trained EEGNet</p><div class="reading">{pct(find('eegnet','wet','wet')!.value)} → {pct(find('eegnet','wet','dry')!.value)}</div><ScoreBar value={Math.abs(wetDrop.difference)} max={0.4} label={`${Math.abs(wetDrop.difference * 100).toFixed(1)} percentage-point loss`} /><p>Moving the same fitted model from wet to dry recordings reduced balanced accuracy by {Math.abs(wetDrop.difference * 100).toFixed(1)} percentage points.</p></article>
      <article class="evidence-card"><p class="eyebrow">Dry-trained EEGNet</p><div class="reading">{pct(find('eegnet','dry','dry')!.value)} → {pct(find('eegnet','dry','wet')!.value)}</div><ScoreBar value={dryGain.difference} max={0.4} tone="olive" label={`${(dryGain.difference * 100).toFixed(1)} percentage-point gain`} /><p>Testing the dry-trained model on wet recordings increased balanced accuracy by {(dryGain.difference * 100).toFixed(1)} points; 95% interval {intervalPp(dryGain.paired_participant_bootstrap_95, 1)}.</p></article>
      <article class="evidence-card"><p class="eyebrow">A different kind of reference</p><div class="reading">{pct(find('fbcca',null,'wet')!.value)} / {pct(find('fbcca',null,'dry')!.value)}</div><ScoreBar value={find('fbcca',null,'dry')!.value} tone="ink" label={`${pct(find('fbcca',null,'dry')!.value)} dry FBCCA balanced accuracy`} /><p>FBCCA reads each target recording analytically. It does not learn from source participants, so it is not a transfer-trained model.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-section" aria-labelledby="references-heading">
    <div class="topic-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Analytic references</p><h2 id="references-heading">No source-training arrow belongs here</h2></div><p>CCA, FBCCA and the power reference operate on the target recording. Listing them once per source sensor would duplicate identical predictions and imply training that never happened.</p></div>
    <div class="topic-table scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Analytic dry and wet reference results, scrolls horizontally">
      <table><caption>Target-recording references on the same two-second, 12-target protocol.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Reference</th><th scope="col">Wet recording</th><th scope="col">Dry recording</th></tr></thead><tbody>{analyticModels.map(model => {
        const wet = find(model, null, 'wet'); const dry = find(model, null, 'dry');
        return <tr><th scope="row">{modelLabel(model)}<small>analytic or fixed reference</small></th><td>{renderMetric(wet)}</td><td>{renderMetric(dry)}</td></tr>;
      })}</tbody></table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-section" id="methods-and-limits" aria-labelledby="limits-heading">
    <div class="topic-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Methods &amp; limits</p><h2 id="limits-heading">What this comparison can support</h2></div><p>These are descriptive intervals over people under a fixed protocol. They are not confidence bounds for all headsets, sessions or model retraining choices.</p></div>
    <div class="method-grid-wide">
      <article><h3>Native wearable evidence</h3><p>This dataset records both sensor types with a native eight-channel headband. It is separate from mobile results that select eight posterior scalp channels from a larger recording.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Order remains a confound</h3><p>Wearing order and time on task are entangled with sensor type. Both order strata show the wet-trained drop, but the design cannot isolate hardware as the sole cause.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Amplitude units are unresolved</h3><p>The release does not document a physical amplitude unit. The scale-dependent foundation-model runs remain held; no unit is guessed here.</p></article>
      <article><h3>One EEGNet seed</h3><p>The source-trained EEGNet cells use one training seed. The paired intervals describe participant variation conditional on that fitted protocol.</p></article>
    </div>
    {citation && <div class="citation-list"><article><h3>{citation.identity.title}</h3><p>{citation.identity.canonical_dataset_citation}</p><p>{citation.identity.paper_citation}</p><p>Aggregate results computed from the official Tsinghua BCI Lab author mirror snapshot acquired 2026-09-20. Local checks do not establish byte identity with Figshare v4.</p><p><a href={citation.primary_sources[0]} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Dataset record ↗</a> · <a href={citation.primary_sources[1]} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Sensors paper ↗</a> · {citation.identity.license}</p></article></div>}
    <p class="citation-note">Data source: <a href="/data/deployment-topics.json" download>reviewed aggregate JSON</a> · schema {data.schema_version} · generated {data.generated_at.slice(0,10)}.</p>
  </section>
</TopicLayout>
